.SH DESCRIPTION
arcv is a lightweight, safe, easy\-to\-use&learn revision control system which leaves the user's original source file tree intact and ensures revisions integrity.
.PP
Unless some traditional well\-known revision control systems, 'arcv' fully respects the user's source folder integrity and does not 'pollute' it with any additional files to achieve revision control. The revision control data are centralized in a dedicated folder, the repository, which can be either a local directory or a mountpoint to a directory located on an external system.
.PP
\&'arcv' only relies on standard system tools and there's no additional command set overlay needed to learn and use, e.g., for adding or removing files. For instance, to exclude and remove a file from the revision control, it is enough to remove the file from the system and commit the new revision. To add a new file, this file just needs to be present before commit.
.PP
The architecture of 'arcv' is readable and open, which enables users to keep full control of the revisions and easily apply common corrective actions without pain. E.g. if the user only recognizes somewhen afterwards that a log message was incomplete or incorrect, it can simply modify the CHANGE_LOG file containing the message, without needing to execute multiple operations and the tooling skills coming along.
.PP
Overall, the above few mentioned features enable 'arcv' to be of an extreme simplicity of use at the reach of anybody, even non\-developers. It enables an instant step\-in and a drastic reduction of the learning curve and daily time overhead generally required to manage revision control.
.PP
AUDIENCE
.IP
arcv can be used by developers for the following purposes:
.IP
\- Individual developers or single person\-lead projects
.IP
\- Early development stages of projects, prototyping
.IP
\- Individual, well\-targeted, independent development lines or branches, which can be easily integrated into the main branch or another revision control system without polluting or unnecessarily complexifying his historical record.
.IP
It can also be used as general\-purpose incremental backup tool.
.PP
PLATFORMS
.IP
Arcv is a bash script primarly developed and tested under Ubuntu.
Because it features only minimalistic dependencies, it is deemed to run on most Linux systems and Cygwin\-enhanced Windows systems.
.PP
DOCUMENTATION
.IP
Please refer to the online page https://slashetc.fr/en/developertoolsforlinux/arcv/arcv__documentation.html to access the full documentation:
.IP
\- Command usage with full examples
.IP
\- Architecture insight
.IP
\- Other pratical information
.PP
SYSTEM DEPENDENCIES
.IP
Arcv automatically handles dependencies toward external system packages, i.e. it attempts to install all required system packages, e.g. meld or tar, when necessary.
.SH "COMMAND DETAILS"
branch BRANCH_FOLDER[:REVISION | RELEASE TAG]
.IP
Branch off a revision.
.PP
changelog
.IP
Display the history log since last release
.PP
check
.IP
Tells whether there are uncommitted modifications. Return 0 when so, 1
otherwise.
.PP
co [REVISION | RELEASE TAG]
.IP
When used without argument, checks out all files of the head revision. Any file
of the current source may get overwritten or removed.
Otherwise, checks out the specified revision, release tag or file from head
revision to the specified output folder.
.PP
destroy
.IP
Put folder out of revision control and delete all related file from repositiory
.PP
diff [(REVISION | head | current)[vs(REVISION | RELEASE TAG)]]
.IP
Makes a diff with the specified revision using the system diff tool.
When no revision number is supplied, the head revision is considered by default.
Returns 0 when no difference is detected, 1 otherwise.
.PP
export [DIRECTORY]
.IP
When used without argument, exports the head revision to the remote Git repo.
When the path to a folder is supplied as argument, exports the content of that
folder to the remote Git repo.
This latter usage is suitable e.g. to export the clean release version of the
source file tree, built at another location.
The URL of the remote Git repo shall be given in GITHUB.txt
.PP
git [(REVISION | head | current)[vs(REVISION | RELEASE TAG)]]
.IP
Makes a diff with the specified revision using git.
When no revision number is supplied, the head revision is considered by default.
.PP
hash [DIRECTORY]
.IP
Displays the hash signature of the head revision when no argument is supplied.
Otherwise, computes the hash signature for the passed directory path
.PP
hook [FILE]
.IP
Without arguments, lists the defined hook(s).
Otherwise, creates a release hook with the passed executable that will be run
whenever a release tag is created. It will ensure that hook as the execution
rights
.PP
list
.IP
Lists the existing source folders stored in the repository
.PP
log [FILENAME]
.IP
Without argument, shows the global commit history of the source folder.
Otherwise, shows the commit history for the specified file.
The global history is always shown from most recent to the oldest in batches of
5.
The file\-specific history is shown from most recent to the oldest as a terminal
menu, from which the changes can be checked against head revision
.PP
meld [(REVISION | head | current)[vs(REVISION | RELEASE TAG)]]
.IP
Makes a diff with the specified revision using meld tool.
When no revision number is supplied, the head revision is considered by default.
.PP
pub [RELEASE TAG:REVISION]
.IP
When no argument is supplied, creates a new release by tagging the head revision
with the content of the VERSION.txt file.
Then it prompts the user for the next version and updates VERSION.txt
accordingly.
.PP
push <arg>
.IP
Pushes data previously fetched to the URI specified with \fB\-\-source\fR.
The data is grabed from the current source folder or the head in the repository.
.PP
rel
.IP
Shows the release log messages from most recent to the oldest
.PP
repo [INFONAME]
.IP
Displays the specified information of the repository. Supported values:
releases latest\-release\-tag latest\-release\-rev exclude\-file exclusions src\-dir
rev\-dir rev\-hashfilepath rev\-hashfilename head\-dir head\-image\-dir  git\-repo size
.PP
rev
.IP
Displays the head revision number
.PP
tarball [REVISION | head]
.IP
Creates a tarball for the current source folder or the specified revision.
When used without argument, creates a tarball for the current source folder,
i.e. the current working directory.
When a revision number is supplied as argument, creates a tarball for the
specified revision.
In both cases, the current working directory must be the root of the versioned
folder.
.SH "OPTIONS DETAILS"
\fB\-\-cm\-cfg\fR YAML path assignment
.IP
Specifies a configuration parameter for the source in the repository.
.IP
The parameter will be stored in the YAML configuration associated with the
source in the repository.
.PP
\fB\-\-config\fR
.IP
Shows the configuration file content
.PP
\fB\-\-cycle\fR INTEGER[+]
.IP
Specifies the size of the revision ring for circular revisions, i.e. the number
of revisions after which the revision number shall wrap around.
.IP
When the cycle is a plain integer, the revision will wrap around starting from 0
again.
.IP
Each saved revision contains all data, and not only the differences.
.IP
At wraparound, a new revision overwrites any previous one of the same number and
thus gets lost.
.IP
When the cycle is an integer number following by +, it additionally initiates a
parallel cycle backup source in the repository. Each time is a cycle is
completed, a new revision is created in the backup source duplicating the last
cycle revision. The name of the cycle backup source is the original source to
which \fB\-cycle\fR is appended and is managed automatically.
.IP
A 0 cycle value means infinite (implicit default value).
.IP
This option can only be specified at the initial commit.
.PP
\fB\-\-debug\fR
.IP
Activate debug logs
.PP
\fB\-\-fetch\fR
.IP
Update the current source folder with the data fetched from the URI specified
with \fB\-\-source\fR and initiate check\-in.
.IP
Note that if the URI was specified at very first commit (creation), it is not
necessary to specify \fB\-\-source\fR at subsequent check\-ins, as the data will be
implicitly fetched from that initial URI.
.IP
This operation is only allowed for a check\-in.
.PP
\fB\-\-fetch\-only\fR
.IP
Creates or updates a source folder that will contain the data fetched from the
passed URI without committing the source folder.
.IP
This operation is only allowed at check\-in, even though no actual check\-in is
initiated afterwards.
.IP
This function is provided for convenience for just generating a standalone
MongoDB dump with dealing with any revision control.
.PP
\fB\-\-files\fR
.IP
Lists the files used by the app, i.e. the configuration file, the log file, the
dependency system package installation cache file
.PP
\fB\-\-fix\fR
.IP
This option enables to explicitly fix the SHA fingerprint aka hash code in case
there's a mismatch between current source and the stored finger whilst no change
was actually detected. This can be useful when the internal hash computation
algorithm may change or could get anyhow broken because of an untested
environment or an internal bug.
In any other case, this option has no effect.
.PP
\fB\-\-force\fR
.IP
Forces a commit even when no change was detected
.PP
\fB\-\-from\fR (REVISION | RELEASE_TAG)
.IP
Specifies the most recent revision or release tag from which to start the
operation. A negative number N designates the Nth revision behind the head. At
the moment, this option is only relevant for the history logs.
.PP
\fB\-\-interval\fR INTEGER[+]
.IP
Specifies the time interval in days, hours, minutes or seconds at which new
revisions are allowed to be created. For instance, if the value is 1h or 3600s,
an attempt to commit a new revision will be rejected an hour after the last
commit. A margin of +\-10s is allowed.
.IP
An explicit value of 0 means there is no restriction (implicit default).
.IP
This option can only be specified at the initial commit and is usually used to
enforce backup strategies.
.PP
\fB\-\-log\fR <arg>
.IP
Show the log tail. By default, shows the last 40 lines and the number of lines
specified as option value.
.PP
\fB\-\-no\-cache\fR, \fB\-U\fR
.IP
Force updating computed data instead of using cached one
.PP
\fB\-\-revive\fR
.IP
Revives a file from the lastest version found in history when the file is
missing in head revision. This option is only valid in the scope of a checkout
operation.
.PP
\fB\-\-rm\fR
.IP
Removes the created source after a direct fetch check\-in
A new revision can be created directly from an URI, without requiring an
existing source folder, by fetching new data from the specified URI. In this
process, a matching source folder is created in the current directory. This
option enables to clean up the folder once the commit completed
.PP
\fB\-\-silent\fR
.IP
Silent mode. Hides messages which are usually displayed even when verbose mode
is not active.
.PP
\fB\-\-source\fR DIRECTORY | <URI>
.IP
Either a source directory to checkout from the repository or an URI from which
to fetch the data from. Currently, only MongoDB URI are supported.
.IP
The source folder to checkout can be specified with this option instead of
resolving the source folder from the current working directory.
.IP
When the value is an URI, it can also be used for the sake of putting under
revision control the data fetched from the URI or create a new revision directly
from the URI without requiring an existing source folder.
.IP
A Mongo database content is fetched below the current workding directory, inside
a source folder of the form "mongodb\-<database name>@<database host>\-<database
port>". The database content is written into a Gzipped archive file is created
of the form "mongo\-db\-<database name>.gz".
.IP
Afterwards, an existing source folder can be used to create new revisions from
the database simply by passing \fB\-\-fetch\fR to the regular arcv commit command (arcv
without argument), because the repository records the original fetch URI.
.PP
\fB\-\-to\fR (REVISION | RELEASE_TAG)
.IP
Specifies the oldest revision or release tag until which to apply the operation.
A negative number N designates the Nth revision behind the head. At the moment,
this option is only relevant for the history logs.
.PP
\fB\-\-verbose\fR, \fB\-V\fR
.IP
Verbose mode
.PP
\fB\-F\fR, \fB\-\-fmt\fR 'adoc' | 'plain'
.IP
Specifies the format of the output of a command. Possible values : "plain" for
raw text without any color nor layout formatting. "adoc" for AsciiDoc format.
.IP
By default when no format is specified, the text may be colored and the text
blocks may be arranged to be more readable.
.IP
At the moment, this option is only relevant for history logs and diffs. There is
no effect if the format is not supported for the applied command
.PP
\fB\-X\fR, \fB\-\-exclude\fR FILE PATTERNS
.IP
Prevent certain file patterns to be checked in.
The file patterns must be supplied as a coma\-separated list of file patterns
.PP
\fB\-f\fR FILENAME
.IP
Arcv operational configuration filename, if different of the default one coming
along with the app, modified or not.
.PP
\fB\-l\fR
.IP
Dry check\-in, listing only new/deleted/changed files
.PP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-message\fR STRING
.IP
Log message
.PP
\fB\-n\fR
.IP
Assume 'No' answer for any confirmation request
.PP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR DIRECTORY
.IP
Output folder for checkouts or tarball exports
.PP
\fB\-y\fR
.IP
Assume 'Yes' answer for any confirmation request
